     6	
     7	Your review saw only the PDF. This time the materials are attached as separate files, so the report, the addendum, the
     8	protocol, the predictions, the ledger, and H4's code and containment note can be checked directly.
     9	
    10	## What changed in substance
    11	
    12	**A. The welfare result was overstated, as you suspected (your Task 2.5).** W1 constrains the mean gain, so an
    13	episode-level lottery between a welfare attack and a profitable attack is feasible. On the evaluation records, a lottery
    14	that plays R3's W0 attack with probability 0.4195 and its H4 attack otherwise keeps the mean gain non-negative and
    15	lowers R3's gross efficiency to 0.932, against 0.997 under the registered W1 search; for C* a lottery with W0 weight
    16	0.4845 reaches 0.921 (`posthoc.json`, Table 11). The paper now says that the profit-constrained welfare
    17	result depends on excluding randomised policies and is not a lower bound. The non-negativity rests on a nominal one-sided 95 percent normal
    18	bound that is not adjusted for selection: the weight and the companion family were chosen on the evaluation seeds, so
    19	this checks the policy class and is not an out-of-sample estimate. The exact weights matter: at a rounded 0.42 the
    20	bound for R3 turns negative.
    21	
    22	**B. The Q1 explanation was wrong (your point 6).** Splitting d = g_C* - g_R3 with the gain's own normalisation into
    23	the attacked-profit and reference-profit terms shows that almost all of d is attacked profit (D2+H3: 0.019 of 0.021;
    24	X7-SC: 0.0225 of 0.024). The paper had said the rules "differ mainly in the attacker's truthful reference profit"; it
