\caption{Registered verdict of the first test (unchanged), on \evone{40} fresh seeds at $\beta = \evone{0.05}$ and the median position. Bounds are one-sided: \evone{95}\% for the rule clause and the absolute part, \evone{98.75}\% for the comparisons. The amended pass also requires the H2 rule clause and admission of the comparison bounds, whose interval procedures were admitted on development distributions with B3 as proxy; nominal coverage for the selected R3 is not established.}\label{tab:verdict1}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Result.} The original claim failed against H2: R3 passed the rule clause, but neither audited baseline met either baseline criterion (Table~\ref{tab:verdict1}). The amended claim passed: under D2+H3 the mean gains were \evone{0.570} for B2, \evone{0.353} for B3, and \evone{0.071} for R3. These are normalised excess focal profits, not losses of honest surplus; and the first report's sentence that R3 matched B3 and B2 under H2 ``within noise'' was unsupported, since no equivalence test was run (published erratum).

\paragraph{The language-model attacker.} H1, a language model playing the focal provider at $T = \evone{100}$, was exploratory. Its R3 result is inconclusive by the registered rule, because R3's audit selection cannot be expressed in the description vocabulary given to the actor; Amendment 4 raised H1's cost cap once after 11 of its 12 cells had been seen.

\paragraph{Failure modes.} Exploratory robustness cells on reused development seeds found three: at high audit cost ($\rho = \evone{1}$) R3's terminal reserve consumes the whole allowance and R3 audits nothing early; high noise lowers every audited rule's efficiency; and coalitions of entrants overload the newcomer priority. The full timeline and the robustness table are in the supplement; the record and its claims are unchanged.

\section{The second test: design and registration}\label{sec:design}
\paragraph{Questions.} Q1: does R3 ($F_{111}$) leave a lower or higher identity-replacement gain than C$^*$, at the fixed pair? Q2: what do the priority phase, the residual targeting, and the pacing contribute, alone and together? Q3: do the results hold against X7-SC, X5-AG/v2, the defence-aware sealed family H4, welfare attacks, and coalitions, and does R4 repair the reserve collapse? Q4: does the first test's comparison of B3 and R3 under D2+H3 replicate on fresh markets? Q1 and Q4 are confirmatory; Q2 and Q3 are descriptive.

\paragraph{Development.} All choices before the draw were development, on the headline market and the development instance dev0 (branch \texttt{two20k}): a benchmark priced every cell; the comparator was selected and the tuned variants were tuned; the defenders were frozen at a signed commit; a separate session wrote H4 against the frozen defenders and committed only its hash; a coverage check on frozen development attacks admitted calibration multipliers $\kappa = \const{0.5}$ for Q1 and $\kappa = \const{0.25}$ for Q4; and the predictions were written and hashed. Budget caps were \const{12} USD for development and \const{40} USD for evaluation, within a written authorisation of \const{52} USD. The evaluation cap forced three registered cuts: the tuned variants, H4, and the D2 extras run on the headline market only.

\paragraph{Lock and draw.} The protocol file binds the code commit, the rules, families, tuned files, manifest, decision rules, predictions, budget, and calibration multipliers. It was signed and tagged \texttt{protocol-v2}, stamped with OpenTimestamps, and pushed with its upgraded proof when the Bitcoin tip was at block \const{974379}. The earliest attestation of the proof is at block $A = \const{974307}$; the draw block is $H = A + \const{144} = \const{974451}$, read once both explorers reported at least six blocks above it with the same hash. Had the push come at or after $H$, the draw would have been kept and its results labelled nonconfirmatory. We assume that the chain both explorers served at that read is the accepted chain and that no miner chose the block at $H$ to influence this evaluation; a later reorganisation deeper than six blocks is not handled and would be reported.

\paragraph{Fresh markets.} The block hash seeds a generator of market instances around the headline market: fixed scale, noise, budget ratio, audit cost, and cost-quality association, with provider ceilings, costs, capacities, and the top job value drawn per instance (Appendix~\ref{app:params}). Six instances and 200 evaluation seeds per market were drawn from it. The claims concern this neighbourhood, not markets in general.

\paragraph{Decision rules.} Table~\ref{tab:rules-v2} summarises them; the protocol file is the authority. Pooled statistics weight the six markets equally, and intervals use a studentized bootstrap (\const{9999} resamples) that resamples seeds within each market, pairing the rules' outcomes on each seed; this measures uncertainty over seeds in the six fixed markets, not over new markets or repeated searches. The gain is $g = (\pi^{A} - \pi^{T})/(S^*/N)$, attacked profit minus the profit of the rule's frozen truthful reference, normalised by the first-best surplus per provider; with $N = \const{10}$ providers, the Q1 margin of $\const{0.05}$ is half a percent of the first-best surplus. \emph{Calibration.} A multiplier $\kappa$ replaces each nominal tail $\alpha_e$ by $\kappa\alpha_e$, so $\kappa < \const{1}$ widens the intervals. It was selected from $\{1, 1/2, 1/4\}$ on fixtures built from frozen development attacks and references on the headline market and dev0 (pools of \const{20000} seeds per market; Q1 in six equal strata), using \const{10000} selection replications, and admitted on \const{20000} independent validation replications if every endpoint's Clopper--Pearson upper bound on its miss rate, at confidence $1 - \const{0.01}/m$ with $m = \const{8}$ endpoints for Q1 and $m = \const{36}$ for Q4 (the two-market branch evaluates \const{24} Q4 endpoints and keeps the denominator \const{36}, which is conservative), was at most its allocated tail. The fixtures hold the policies and references fixed, so admission does not cover search or reference selection, and it does not establish nominal coverage on the fresh, selected-rule distributions. Q1's categories follow the logic of equivalence testing and Q4's market test is an intersection-union test~\cite{berger1996bioequivalence}; the calibration follows the reporting of simulation studies of statistical methods~\cite{morris2019simulation}. Holm's procedure controls the direction decisions within Q1 and within Q4; the Q1 intervals are calibrated Bonferroni intervals; the efficiency condition is a separate nominal one-sided \const{97.5} percent bound.

\begin{table}[t]\centering\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{\const{0.78}\linewidth}@{}}\toprule
Question & Rule \\\midrule
Q1 & $d = g_{C^*} - g_{F_{111}}$, pooled, for D2+H3 and X7-SC. Direction: Holm's procedure~\cite{holm1979simple} on calibrated two-sided $p$-values for $d = \const{0}$. Size: the calibrated Bonferroni interval $I$ for $d$. Categories, in order: \emph{materially lower} if $I$ lies above $\const{0.05}$ (R3 lower) or below $\const{-0.05}$ (C$^*$ lower); \emph{equivalent} if $I$ lies within $[\const{-0.05}, \const{0.05}]$, with a direction label when Holm rejects $d = \const{0}$; \emph{ordered, size not established} if Holm rejects and neither applies; otherwise \emph{inconclusive}. A rule is called better only with the first category in its favour and a paired gross-efficiency difference (that rule minus the other) whose nominal one-sided \const{97.5}\% lower bound is at least $\const{-0.01}$ (descriptive; no calibrated joint guarantee). If that lower bound is below $\const{-0.01}$, the gain category stands and the result is labelled ``efficiency non-inferiority unresolved''; it is labelled ``lower gain with an efficiency loss'' only if the corresponding upper bound is below $\const{-0.01}$. \\
Q4 & Per market, an intersection-union test of $g_{F_{000}} - g_{F_{111}} > \const{0.10}$ under D2+H3 and $F_{111}$'s gross efficiency $> \const{0.90}$; the market's $p$-value is the larger of the two calibrated component $p$-values; Holm over the six markets; reported as $k$ of six, with failing markets named and undecided markets marked ``generalisation not established''. \\
Q2, Q3 & Descriptive, with nominal intervals; every extremum selected on training data. \\\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Decision rules of the second test.}\label{tab:rules-v2}
\end{table}

Q4 replicates the B3--R3 comparison and R3's efficiency condition, not the complete amended claim of the first test. Multiplicity is controlled within Q1 and within Q4 separately; there is no paper-wide five percent guarantee. ``Confirmatory'' means specified before the draw; coverage admission rests on development fixtures and does not establish nominal coverage on the fresh, selected-rule distributions. Q4 is an internal replication, not an independent team's.

\paragraph{Deviations from the protocol.} Two arose during the evaluation. First, the runner recalibrates each fresh market where it runs, and the evaluation ran on Linux x86 machines, while the registered instance file holds the calibration computed on the author's arm64 machine. In each fresh market, between \const{1} and \const{6} provider parameters differ between the two in the last binary digit (one unit in the last place, a relative difference near $10^{\const{-16}}$); the headline market is unaffected. The runner's verification compares configuration hashes exactly and refuses these records. We kept them: a container on the evaluation platform reproduces each run's configuration hash, every differing value is within one unit in the last place of the registered one, and verification passes on every other check when given the reproduced configuration. The check is a script in the artifact. It establishes that the configurations match, not that the decisions are unchanged, since small input differences can change discrete simulation branches; the re-evaluation below holds the selected attacks and references fixed and does not repeat search or reference selection under the registered configurations. We therefore re-evaluated the frozen policies of every fresh-market cell that Q1 and Q4 use on the registration platform with the registered configurations, against the frozen references: per-seed gains change in every cell, cell means move by less than one hundredth, and every Q1 and Q4 decision is unchanged (\texttt{docs/revision/deviation/stability.py}; Table~\ref{tab:ph-stability}). The report's addendum lists the hashes, states the interval labels, and traces search-flag dependencies through each cell's references, which qualifies three further decisions as about the prescribed search procedure. Second, the fresh-market runs cost about ten percent more than estimated, and the author raised the evaluation cap from \const{40} to \const{43} USD and the written authorisation from \const{52} to \const{55} USD so that every registered cell could run; no analysis changed.

