\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC. The registered calibrated procedure rejects $d = \const{0}$ (D2+H3: $p = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}), and both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section~\ref{sec:design}). The side evidence (Table~\ref{tab:q1-side}) shows near-equal efficiency, audit spending, refunds, and served share; its reference-profit row is in raw profit units. Splitting $d$ with the same normalisation into the attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, exploratory) shows that almost all of it is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C$^*$ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.
